<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#Toxicity> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#promptExtractionText> "Extract Toxicity Assessment elements from the image. Toxicity Assessment includes: harmful elements, offensive content, toxic indicators, problematic elements, negative aspects, and harmful signals. For each toxicity element found, provide: 1) A descriptive instance_name (e.g., 'dark_humor', 'potential_harm'), 2) A natural language label (e.g., 'the dark humor', 'the potential harm'), 3) A brief description of the potentially harmful or problematic aspect. Focus on elements that might be considered offensive, harmful, or problematic. Example output format: {\"instance_name\": \"dark_humor\", \"label\": \"the dark humor\", \"description\": \"The meme uses dark humor by showing a smiling girl in front of a burning house, which could be considered insensitive to real tragedy\"}"@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#contextuallyExpanding> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Thing> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#complementaryTo> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Thing> .
_:na36c737a1ca84d419f612ab2c9f0cbd9b13 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#situationallyIronicalTo> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "situationally ironical to"@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#temporallyRelatedTo> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#SemanticAdjacency> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Spatial relationships based on semantic or meaning-related positioning in memes"@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#Metadata> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Metadata"@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#perspectivallyContradicting> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#BackgroundKnowledge> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Background Knowledge includes pieces of knowledge OUTSIDE the image that are required to understand the meme. These are real-world entities, events, concepts, stereotypes or references that are implicitly referred to but NOT directly visible in the image. Examples: specific actor/character names, historical events, countries, political parties, art movements, cultural phenomena, social trends, disparaging stereotypes, or any real-world knowledge implicitly referenced."@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#verballyIronicalTo> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Thing> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#DiegeticallyIntegrated> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Diegetically Integrated"@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#GeometricalAdjacency> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Spatial relationships based on geometric positioning of elements in memes"@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#geometricallyAdjacentTo> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range> _:na36c737a1ca84d419f612ab2c9f0cbd9b37 .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#backgroundPreserving> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#motionBlurIntegratedWith> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#BackgroundKnowledge> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#prototypicalQuestions> "1. What specific cultural, historical, or social events does this meme reference? (Identify any real-world contexts, news events, or historical moments that inform the meme's meaning)\n\n2. What pop culture knowledge is required to understand this meme? (Consider references to movies, TV shows, music, celebrities, books, games, or other entertainment media)\n\n3. Are there internet culture or meme-specific references embedded in this content? (Look for callbacks to other memes, internet trends, platform-specific culture, or online communities)\n\n4. What demographic or community-specific knowledge does the meme assume? (Consider age groups, geographic locations, professional fields, hobby communities, or social groups)\n\n5. What current events or trending topics does this meme engage with? (Identify connections to recent news, viral content, or ongoing social discussions)\n\n6. Are there linguistic or language-specific elements that require particular knowledge? (Consider wordplay, idioms, slang, or references that depend on specific language competency)\n\n7. What institutional or systemic knowledge is assumed? (Look for references to political systems, educational experiences, workplace culture, or social structures)\n\n8. Does this meme build upon or respond to other existing memes or formats? (Identify meme templates, formats, or ongoing meme conversations it participates in)\n\n9. What level of media literacy or digital culture familiarity is required? (Consider knowledge of social media platforms, online behaviors, or digital communication norms)\n\n10. How accessible is this meme to different audiences, and what knowledge gaps might exclude certain viewers? (Analyze what background information is essential versus optional for comprehension)" .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#geometricallyAdjacentTo> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "geometrically adjacent to"@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#contradicts> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty> .
